      "title": "Triad Inversions and Bass Notes",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** chords | **Difficulty:** 3/10 | **Time:** ~10 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Inversions rearrange which chord tone sits in the bass without changing the chord's identity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "three-positions",
      "title": "Three positions",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Root position**: root in bass (C-E-G). **First inversion**: third in bass, written C/E. **Second inversion**: fifth in bass, written C/G. The chord symbol reflects root and quality; slash notation shows bass note."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "voice-leading",
      "title": "Voice leading",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Inversions create smoother bass lines: C → G/B → Am → G/C descends stepwise. Figured bass uses 6 (first inversion) and 6/4 (second inversion) in classical sources."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "session-use",
      "title": "Session use",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Choose inversions by ear for smooth bass motion and to keep the melody clear of the accompaniment register."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
